Subject: nanodregg replaced bubblewrap with self-confinement: a confined child applies Landlock (filesystem, ABI 6 on the target kernel), seccomp (a syscall allowlist) and no-new-privileges to itself at startup, and receives its sockets and directory descriptors from the launcher through a connector instead of opening paths. Design: /Users/ember/dev/redregg/work/promo/DESIGN-SANDBOX.md. Code: on GitHub main at commit 7ec9456 (read via `ssh -n hbox 'cd /tank/dregg-build/nanodregg/main && git fetch -q origin && git show origin/main --stat'` and `git show origin/main:<path>` for sandbox/src/*.rs, launcher/src/*.rs, decisions/client.md's CLIENT-48 entry). Context: the processes confined are the writer (`host serve`, our own Lean code compiled to native) and the resident; the OS sandbox is defence in depth; the kernel's own grant system confines what a member or agent may do. The deployment runs under systemd's hardening (RestrictNamespaces, ProtectKernelTunables, ProcSubset=pid, RestrictSUIDSGID, SystemCallFilter=@system-service @sandbox, RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX, NoExecPaths). The reason bwrap was dropped: it needed user namespaces and the new mount API, which that hardening forbids, and RestrictSUIDSGID's seccomp rule rejects openat2 wholesale, which bwrap 0.12 depends on.

